**Read:** [Experimental Methods in Fluid Mechanics — Pranab K. Mondal](../sources/Experimental-Methods-in-Fluids-Mondal.lingua#r_4XFYEGQVB1EV4QF9BP17ME04Y4), measurement analysis and data interpretation; [Lectures on Non-Newtonian Fluid Dynamics — John Hinch](../sources/Non-Newtonian-Fluid-Dynamics-Hinch.lingua#r_06KY7WE9WYAF856YQKQXHY1DS3), rheometric inference and constitutive comparison; [CFD verification and validation — NPARC Alliance](../sources/CFD-Verification-and-Validation-NPARC.lingua#r_7P72ZWZEJ0SVMWFDGK52N9AZPV), validation uncertainty and model assessment.

Inverting a pressure drop to find viscosity requires geometry, flow and boundary conditions to be known well enough. If both radius and viscosity are free in the laminar pipe relation, one pressure-flow datum cannot identify them separately. Similar ambiguity appears between roughness and local losses, inlet turbulence and closure constants, or wall slip and material response.

Write the forward model and its sensitivity to each parameter. Examine units, correlations, near-degenerate directions and prior constraints. Use several conditions or measurement types that change those sensitivities differently. Keep calibration data distinct from evidence reserved to test the prediction.

Compare an inferred parameter with an independent physical measurement and report the interval supported by the data and model. A narrow numerical fit interval can be misleading if model discrepancy is omitted. State what experiment would resolve an ambiguity and which conclusions remain possible even without identifying every internal parameter.

